                <person xml:id="LAN11" sex="1">
                    <persName>
                        <roleName/>
                        <forename>William Balcombe</forename>
                        <surname>Langridge</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1757">
                        <placeName/>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1845"/>
                    <occupation>solicitor</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Langridge was a solicitor in Lewes, Sussex whom Godwin meets in February 1804. Godwin had travelled there on behalf of his brother 
    <persName key="GOD02">Joseph</persName> whose daughter <persName key="GOD06">Harriet</persName> has run away with <persName key="WES06">Thomas West</persName>. 
    Langridge appears to have been some sort of mediator in the affair. But it is curious that someone who appears only twice and in what 
  seems to be a personal affair should be recorded in the 1796 list. </p>
                    </note>
